gotochanger (0.6.2) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * Fix kernel mode never moving a volume's end-of-data back when it is
    overwritten, so rewinding and relabelling a recorded tape left the
    whole previous pass on it. WRITE(6) wrote at the current position
    without truncating, and WRITE FILEMARKS(6) only ever appended to the
    filemark sidecar, yet end-of-data is read straight off the backing
    file's size - so on real hardware a `mt rewind; mt weof` followed by a
    Bareos `label barcodes` against a 3.1GB volume left all 3.1GB in place
    behind the fresh 64512-byte label. Bareos's own space-to-end-of-data
    before an append then landed 3.1GB in, while the label's trailing
    filemark sat at 64512, so a job written there could not be positioned
    to by file number; the volume also still reported 3.1GB of its 12GB
    used immediately after being labelled, and a second attempt to erase
    it by hand failed with "Cannot label Volume because it is already
    labeled", because writing a filemark never touched the label block
    either. Both commands now set end-of-data where the write ended, as
    real tape does, which also makes the standard `mt rewind; mt weof`
    erase idiom work. WRITE FILEMARKS with a zero transfer length is
    excluded - it writes nothing to the medium and is the conventional
    buffer flush.

 -- Sebastien WENSKE <sebastien@wenske.fr>  Sun, 26 Jul 2026 22:00:00 +0000

gotochanger (0.6.1) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * SECURITY: restrict manual backup download (GET /api/v1/backup/download)
    to Admin - it was Operator+, on the once-correct basis that a backup
    holds only virtual-tape-library state. That stopped being true when
    user accounts and API tokens moved out of users.json/tokens.json and
    into the same state.db a backup snapshots with VACUUM INTO, so any
    operator could download every admin's PBKDF2 password hash for offline
    cracking. Stripping those tables from the operator's copy was
    considered and rejected: a stripped database, once restored, comes
    back with an empty users table, which puts the daemon into "bootstrap
    required" - i.e. whoever reaches the web UI first sets the new admin
    password. Behavior change for operators: the Admin section was
    previously reachable by Operator+ purely because Backup lived in it,
    so the whole Admin nav is now Admin-only.
  * SECURITY: require Admin on all five /api/v1/wizard* endpoints. They
    were registered with no role requirement at all, commented "public for
    initial setup", and nothing ever revoked that once setup finished - so
    on a configured, running deployment any unauthenticated caller that
    could reach the TCP listener was able to read the full library
    topology (GET /api/v1/wizard) and rewrite it, or reset the wizard
    (POST /api/v1/wizard/reset, which answered 204). The exemption was
    never needed: the web UI only calls these after /api/v1/auth/state
    reports authenticated, and both bootstrap and login establish an Admin
    session first. gotochangerctl and the Bareos shim are unaffected - the
    trusted Unix socket presents every request as admin.
  * Fix every volume sent offsite being silently lost on the next daemon
    restart. Library.State() and saveLocked() each built the persisted
    state struct field by field, independently, and had drifted:
    saveLocked simply omitted OffsiteVolumes. Since OffsiteSend clears the
    source slot and moves the cartridge into the offsite list alone, the
    persisted state recorded it as existing nowhere at all - the backing
    file survived on disk with nothing referencing it. Stayed invisible
    because State(), the correct one, has no non-test callers: every test
    that round-tripped state exercised it while production used the broken
    one. Both now share a single stateLocked() builder.
  * Fix a drive's origin slot being dropped on daemon restart, so
    gotochanger-changer's "loaded"/"listall" reported slot 0 to Bareos for
    any tape still mounted at restart - losing track of which slot to
    unload it back into. Drive.Origin was persisted correctly but never
    copied back by restore(). Fixed alongside a second defect in the same
    function: it matched persisted placements by array position behind an
    all-or-nothing "only if the element counts still match" guard, so any
    topology change while the daemon was down (a magazine resized from 5
    to 10 slots) silently discarded *every* volume placement rather than
    just the affected ones. It now matches by address/physical index, the
    way Library.Reconfigure always has.
  * Fix the reader half of the 0.6.0 "concurrent map iteration and map
    write" crash. That release gave the SNMP notifier goroutine its own
    copy of Event.Detail, but Events() still handed callers the stored
    maps themselves, and Status()/LogicalLibraryStatus()/
    ListLogicalLibraries()/GetLogicalLibrary()/UnassignedElements()/
    AllVolumes() still returned the live slot/ioslot/drive/volume
    pointers - all of which the HTTP handlers JSON-marshal *after*
    releasing the library lock. A marshal could therefore read a
    Slot.Volume pointer or a Volume's written-bytes field at the moment
    Load/Unload/the capacity poll wrote it. These are now genuine deep
    snapshots, as their doc comments always claimed; confirmed by tests
    that report real data races against the previous code.
  * Fix gotochanger-changer being unable to address an I/O (import/export)
    slot at all: load, unload, offsite-send and offsite-recall passed a
    hardcoded element kind of "slot" alongside an address that can just as
    legitimately resolve to an I/O slot - storage and I/O addresses share
    one contiguous range by design, "slots" reports the combined total,
    and "listall" advertises the I/O ones with an I: prefix. Any Bareos
    operation against a mail slot therefore failed with "element not
    found", for an address this binary itself had advertised. They now
    resolve the kind from the address, as "transfer" already did.
  * Fix the Admin Settings API writing stale values back over the setup
    wizard's. Settings caches the configuration it was constructed with at
    daemon startup and persists all of it on any update, but the wizard
    writes vtl_name/offsite_location straight to the database, and nothing
    refreshed that cache - so the first settings save of any kind, even
    one touching only the log level, reverted them to their boot-time
    values (an empty VTL name, on a fresh install). The read side was
    wrong too: Admin > Settings and a manual backup's filename both showed
    the stale name.
  * Raise the CLI/API client's request timeout from 15s to 10 minutes
    (overridable). It was shorter than this project's own factory-default
    simulated latency: closing a magazine door at stock settings takes 20s
    (2s door + 6s arm + 12s scan) while holding the library lock, so
    `gotochangerctl storage-door close` timed out against a completely
    healthy daemon. No shorter value is derivable - each delay is tunable
    up to 5m, and the single-arm model queues every other operation behind
    whichever one holds the lock.
  * Remove dead code (an unused static-asset predicate, a parallel
    WizardProgress store type nothing ever called, an empty internal/web
    directory), sort the outside-tape inventory deterministically instead
    of leaving it in Go map order (it reshuffled in the dashboard on every
    door close), and fix two files failing gofmt.

 -- Sebastien WENSKE <sebastien@wenske.fr>  Sun, 26 Jul 2026 21:00:00 +0000

gotochanger (0.6.0) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * Package the TCMU/LIO kernel-mode backend for the first time: a new
    gotochanger-kernel binary package (Depends: gotochanger) ships
    gotochanger-tcmud, a templated gotochanger-tcmud@.service systemd
    unit, and a modules-load.d conf that loads target_core_mod/
    target_core_user/tcm_loop at boot. Built and real-hardware-verified
    in-repo since the previous session (see CLAUDE.md's "Kernel mode
    (TCMU/LIO)" section); this is what makes it installable rather than
    a source-only binary. Not enabled by default - an admin runs
    `systemctl enable --now gotochanger-tcmud@<logical-library-or-
    default>.service` once ready, per logical library (or @default for
    the whole physical library). Unlike gotochanger.service, the new
    unit runs unsandboxed as root, which TCMU configfs/UIO/netlink setup
    genuinely requires.
  * Add --logical-library scoping to gotochanger-tcmud, mirroring
    gotochanger-changer's own flag - a deployment with multiple logical
    libraries now runs one gotochanger-tcmud instance per logical
    library, each with its own changer LUN and only that library's
    drives, the kernel-mode equivalent of one Autochanger per logical
    library in changer-script mode.
  * Fix gotochanger-tcmud resolving a drive's SCSI identity/native speed
    from its DriveType catalog *name* instead of its Generation field -
    coincidentally correct against the stock drive-type catalog (every
    entry is named after its own generation) but silently wrong for any
    custom-named drive type, which would fall back to a generic identity
    instead of matching its real family.
  * Kernel mode's MOVE MEDIUM now reports cause-specific SCSI sense data
    for a drive/robotic fault (NOT READY, manual intervention required)
    and a cleaning-tape failure (CLEANING FAILURE), instead of the same
    generic mechanical-positioning-or-changer-error sense for every
    possible failure.
  * The wizard's kernel-mode radio button (previously permanently
    disabled) is now live-gated by a new GET /api/v1/kernel-mode/status
    check (is the package installed, is the kernel module loaded), with
    an inline hint when it isn't available yet. Admin > Logical
    Libraries gained a "Kernel Mode Setup" button (next to the existing
    "Bareos Config" one) printing the exact systemctl command for that
    logical library, plus a whole-library equivalent for a deployment
    with no logical libraries configured.
  * Fix a data race between Library.emit's async SNMP-notify goroutine
    and AnnotateEventsSince, both accessing the same Event.Detail map
    with no synchronization - crashed gotochangerd on bareos-disk-sd-
    int-fr1 with "concurrent map iteration and map write" (systemd
    restarted it within 2s, no lasting outage). Fixed by giving the
    notifier goroutine its own deep copy of Detail (cloneEventForNotify).
  * Add gotochanger-tcmud device-path reporting: a new
    POST/DELETE /api/v1/kernel-mode/devices/{instance} and
    GET /api/v1/kernel-mode/devices (in-memory only, not persisted)
    let each running gotochanger-tcmud instance report its real
    /dev/sg*/nst* paths back to gotochangerd once discovered, so
    Admin > Drives, Admin > Logical Libraries, and the generated Bareos
    Config show the real kernel-assigned device instead of the
    changer-script-mode symlink once kernel mode is actually running.
    Also fixes a latent bug where gotochanger-tcmud built each SCSI
    drive with its loop position instead of its real physical drive
    index, which only ever worked by coincidence for a physically
    contiguous-from-0 logical library.
  * Fix two logical libraries' kernel-mode backstores/loopback WWNs
    colliding under the shared default HBA - every instance named its
    changer/drive backstores with bare, non-unique names ("changer0",
    "drive0", ...), so a second logical library's instance could create
    its own directory but could never enable it (the kernel silently
    reused the first instance's already-registered object), retrying
    forever. Fixed by prefixing every backstore/WWN with the owning
    instance's own name (its logical library, or "default").
  * Fix gotochanger-tcmud not picking up a drive reassigned between
    logical libraries while its instance keeps running - it resolves
    its own drive set once, at startup, so a live Admin > Logical
    Libraries edit had no effect until someone manually restarted the
    affected instances. The gotochangerd reconciler now compares each
    running instance's last self-reported drive set against the live
    topology and restarts it (stop then start, matching the existing
    narrowly-scoped polkit grant) when they disagree. Also fixes a
    related cross-process race in gotochanger-tcmud's own SCSI-host
    discovery: two instances restarting at the same moment could each
    grab the wrong newly-created /dev/sg* host, now serialized via a
    new cross-process flock.
  * gotochanger-kernel now depends on polkitd explicitly (Debian
    trixie's current package name for what used to be policykit-1) -
    without it actually installed, every systemctl call the automatic
    gotochanger-tcmud instance start/stop reconciler makes has no
    polkit authority to consult and silently falls back to
    default-deny, so kernel mode would pass every "is it available"
    check yet never actually start anything. Found on a minimal
    bareos-disk-sd-int-fr1 install that never had polkitd for
    unrelated reasons; the dev host this feature was built against
    already had it, which is why this stayed hidden.
  * Fix a real-kernel race in gotochanger-tcmud's own device-path
    discovery: it read the device/generic symlink exactly once,
    immediately after triggering an asynchronous SCSI bus rescan, with
    no wait - fine on the original dev host, but a medium-changer
    peripheral device measurably takes longer to finish registering
    than a tape peripheral device does on at least one real kernel,
    making the changer's own path discovery fail 100% of the time while
    every drive succeeded. Fixed with a short, bounded retry.
  * Implement MODE SENSE(6) page 0x1D (Element Address Assignment) for
    the emulated changer. Real mtx (and Bareos's own bundled
    mtx-changer script, which wraps it) queries this page as part of
    its status probe; without it, mtx fails the query and falls back to
    a full scan, printing a diagnostic to stderr along the way - and
    Bareos SD's own AutochangerCmd() reads only the *first* line of the
    combined child-process output for a "slots" command, so it parsed
    mtx's own diagnostic text as the slot count (0) instead of the real
    number. Found by testing with real mtx against real hardware for
    the first time in this project's history - prior verification
    passes only ever used hand-crafted sg_raw CDBs, which never
    exercised this code path.
  * Fix READ ELEMENT STATUS/REQUEST VOLUME ELEMENT ADDRESS never
    setting the PVolTag bit in the element status page header, so
    barcode data was genuinely present in the response bytes but any
    real, spec-compliant initiator (including Bareos's own barcode
    scan) correctly ignored it as absent. Found immediately after the
    MODE SENSE fix above, via the same real-mtx testing pass.
  * Fix internal/scsi.Drive.volume() indexing the drive list by
    physical drive index instead of searching it by that field -
    correct only when a gotochanger-tcmud instance happens to expose
    the *entire* physical drive list (array position equals physical
    index), which silently broke every real drive-level SCSI command
    (TEST UNIT READY, LOAD/UNLOAD, READ/WRITE(6), REWIND, SPACE(6),
    LOCATE(10)) for any --logical-library-scoped instance whose drives
    don't start at physical index 0. The resulting NOT READY response
    made a real initiator's own open()/TEST UNIT READY retry loop
    (Linux's st driver, inside a real backup job) hang indefinitely
    instead of failing - not a hang in this project's own code, but one
    it silently caused. Found on bareos-disk-sd-int-fr1's Library2
    (drives at physical index 4-5) after Library1 (drives at 0-3, where
    the bug is invisible) had already tested clean.
  * Implement filemark support for the kernel-mode drive backend:
    WRITE FILEMARKS(6) (previously entirely unimplemented, which made
    every real backup job fail at end-of-volume with an I/O error from
    Bareos's own MTWEOF), SPACE(6) code=1 (filemark-count positioning,
    needed for restore/copy jobs to seek to a specific file), and
    filemark-aware READ(6) truncation. Filemark positions are tracked
    in a small per-volume sidecar file, never touched by the
    byte-for-byte backing file gotochangerd's own activity-watcher/
    capacity-poll already depend on. Also fixed two bugs found while
    verifying this against a real kernel: TCMU's UFLAG_READ_LEN flag
    was never actually set on a completion, silently zeroing out any
    short/partial read regardless of cause (not filemark-specific - it
    affected every previously-shipped short-read/ILI response too); and
    a plain sequential multi-file write was deleting the very filemark
    separating it from the file before it, since that filemark sits
    exactly at the next file's own start position.
  * Add real, stable /dev/tape/by-id/scsi-3<wwn>[-nst] device paths for
    kernel mode by implementing SCSI INQUIRY VPD pages 0x00 (Supported
    Pages) and 0x83 (Device Identification) - the kernel's own raw
    /dev/sg*/nst* numbering is not stable across a gotochanger-tcmud
    restart, which broke Bareos config that hard-coded them. No
    gotochanger-side udev rules were needed: systemd's own stock
    persistent-storage-tape rules already build exactly this style of
    symlink from a real VPD 0x83 query, the same mechanism the real ML3
    hardware this project models already uses. Kernel-mode device
    reporting, Admin > Drives/Logical Libraries, and the generated
    Bareos Config now all prefer this stable path over the raw one.
  * Fix gotochanger-tcmud@<name> instances not restarting when
    gotochangerd itself restarts (a redeploy, a crash-and-restart),
    leaving stale device reports and outdated code running until an
    admin noticed and restarted them by hand. The reconciler now forces
    a restart of an already-active, still-desired instance specifically
    on gotochangerd's own startup, without affecting its normal,
    narrower behavior (only touch an instance whose drive set actually
    changed) for ordinary topology edits.
  * Rename the Go module path from the old GitLab location
    (gitlab.com/internal-org/foundation/products/infrastructure/packages/
    gotochanger) to github.com/swenske/gotochanger, following the
    repository's move - updates every internal import plus the
    Homepage/Source metadata in debian/control, debian/copyright, and
    the SNMP MIB's CONTACT-INFO.

 -- Sebastien WENSKE <sebastien@wenske.fr>  Sun, 26 Jul 2026 18:30:00 +0000

gotochanger (0.5.0) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * Rework latency simulation: replace the fixed none/low/medium/high
    profile catalog with a fully custom set of 7 numeric delays (drive
    load/unload, tape positioning inside the drive, robotic arm movement
    for tape moves and for magazine scans, tape scanning after a
    magazine is closed, door open/close movement), editable live from a
    new Admin > Latency page (with a "Load defaults" prefill) and the
    matching GET/PUT /api/v1/settings/latency API/gotochangerctl latency
    subcommand - previously only settable once, during the setup wizard,
    from 4 fixed presets, with no way to retune afterwards short of a
    raw REST call against an undocumented field. The wizard's step 8 is
    now just an "Enable tape library latency simulation" checkbox; the
    actual values always come from Admin > Latency (or the seeded
    factory defaults). Applies to the whole physical library, so every
    logical library sharing one gotochangerd instance shares one timing
    model, same as before. Upgrading a database that has an existing
    latency_profile setting migrates it into the new fields instead of
    silently resetting to disabled (see migrateLegacyLatencySetting).
  * Add robotics fault simulation: a new POST /api/v1/robotics/fault
    endpoint (operator+, same role as the existing per-drive fault
    toggle) and dashboard "Robotic Arm" panel let an operator raise a
    simulated fault on the library's single robotic arm, with a
    realistic kind (blocked arm, mispositioned cartridge, cartridge
    pickup failure, cartridge drop/transport failure, movement jam, or
    other) and an optional message. While active, Move/Load/Unload are
    rejected (409); magazine/mailbox door open/close remain available.
    Only one robotic action can run at a time and a second one waits for
    the first to finish (already true of the existing per-operation
    locking, not new infrastructure). Fault state persists across a
    daemon restart, same as the existing per-drive fault. Also available
    via gotochangerctl robotic-fault on|off.
  * Fix the Admin > Logical Libraries "Bareos Config" generator omitting
    the Drive Index directive from generated Device{} blocks. Real
    Bareos SD defaults Drive Index to 0 for any Device that doesn't set
    it explicitly, so an Autochanger with 2+ drives had every drive
    silently collapse to "drive 0" from Bareos's point of view. Found on
    bareos-disk-sd-int-fr1: mounting slot 2 into drive 1 actually loaded
    it into physical drive 0. Fixed by emitting Drive Index set to each
    drive's 0-based rank within that logical library (matching what
    gotochanger-changer's --logical-library scoping expects), not
    gotochangerd's own drive index. README.md's Bareos integration
    example had the same gap and got the same fix. Does not fix
    bareos-disk-sd-int-fr1's live Ansible-managed config - see
    CLAUDE.md's "Known gotchas" #13.

