gotochanger (0.3.2) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * Fix the drive-index companion to the 0.3.1 slot-addressing fix:
    gotochanger-changer looked up drives by gotochangerd's global physical
    index, but Bareos computes its "drive N" argument as the 0-based
    position of the Device within that Autochanger's own Device list, not
    the global index. A logical library whose drives aren't the
    physically-lowest-indexed ones (e.g. Library2's Drive4/Drive5) got
    "drive 0 not found" for what Bareos considers its first drive.
    presentedAddressing now renumbers drive indices the same way it
    already renumbers slot addresses - identical to today's behavior when
    unscoped (global indices are already dense from 0), only different
    when scoped to a logical library. Found immediately after deploying
    0.3.1 to bareos-disk-sd-int-fr1's Library2/TAPES-EXPORT.

 -- Sebastien WENSKE <sebastien@wenske.fr>  Wed, 15 Jul 2026 17:00:00 +0000

gotochanger (0.3.1) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * Fix gotochanger-changer leaking global physical slot/I-O-slot addresses
    to Bareos when an Autochanger is scoped to a logical library via
    --logical-library. A logical library's elements can sit at arbitrary,
    non-contiguous physical addresses (e.g. a second logical library
    carved out of magazines/mailboxes added after the first), but Bareos's
    per-Storage-resource "max slots" bookkeeping assumes element addresses
    never exceed the total slot count, so anything beyond the first
    logical library's own range got silently dropped ("Slot N greater
    than max M ignored"). gotochanger-changer now renumbers every address
    it shows to (or accepts from) Bareos into a dense 1-based range scoped
    to whatever's in view for the current invocation - identical to the
    existing physical addressing when unscoped (the whole library is
    already dense from 1), only different when scoped to a logical
    library. Found on bareos-disk-sd-int-fr1's second logical library
    (Library2 / TAPES-EXPORT), whose slots physically sit at 41-80 with
    I/O slots at 106-110.

 -- Sebastien WENSKE <sebastien@wenske.fr>  Wed, 15 Jul 2026 15:30:00 +0000

gotochanger (0.3.0) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * Move the last remaining service-level settings that used to live in
    config.yaml (snmp, poll_interval, log_level, tokens_file, users_file)
    into the database, editable live through the Admin Settings API/UI.
    config.yaml is now down to just data_dir and listen - the two settings
    that genuinely have to be known before the database can even be
    opened. Settings.Update no longer rewrites config.yaml at all.
  * Add database backup/restore: Admin > Backup in the web UI, a
    `gotochangerctl backup`/`restore` CLI, and a REST API
    (/api/v1/backup/download, /api/v1/backups, /api/v1/backup/schedule,
    /api/v1/restore). A backup is a consistent snapshot of the whole
    database (topology, settings, dynamic element/volume state) taken with
    SQLite's `VACUUM INTO`, named "<vtl_name>_<timestamp>.db". Operator+
    can trigger and download a manual backup; Admin can additionally
    schedule recurring backups (interval + retention) and restore from an
    uploaded backup file, both in Admin > Backup and in wizard step 1 (to
    skip configuring from scratch). Restore replaces the database file and
    deliberately exits non-zero so systemd's Restart=on-failure brings the
    daemon back up against it - it does not attempt to hot-swap the
    running Library, unlike every other topology change in this release.
    User accounts and API tokens are intentionally out of scope for
    backup/restore: they're not "virtual tape library" state, and stay in
    their existing separate JSON files.

